WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.467
The mean.

2
00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:04.057
It's the first number everyone reaches for.

3
00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:06.667
Let's see what it really is, and where it breaks.

4
00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.467
The mean is the sum of the values divided by how many there are.

5
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.050
Picture the values sitting on a seesaw.

6
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.333
The mean is where the fulcrum balances.

7
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:22.533
It also minimizes squared distance to every point, which is why squared-error models predict means.

8
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.833
Here are five values on a number line.

9
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.311
They balance at three.

10
00:00:27.311 --> 00:00:28.167
That's the mean.

11
00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:31.533
Now watch what happens when one extreme value joins.

12
00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:36.600
A single nine shifts the fulcrum to four, away from where the crowd actually sits.

13
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.133
So when can you trust it?

14
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:42.533
For symmetric, well behaved data, the mean is great.

15
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:48.067
For skewed data like salaries or latencies, a few extremes drag it away from the crowd.

16
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.733
The reason: every value pulls on the mean in proportion to its distance.

17
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:55.067
Let's see it in code.

18
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:58.180
Ten rocky planet masses, in Earth masses.

19
00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:02.433
Their mean is zero point nine six five, a fair typical planet.

20
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:05.247
Now one Jupiter sneaks into the sample.

21
00:01:05.247 --> 00:01:09.500
The mean jumps to nearly thirty, which describes no planet at all.

22
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.200
Here's a real dashboard.

23
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.121
Most users see a response in about nine tenths of a second.

24
00:01:16.121 --> 00:01:19.067
The mean reads one point four, which looks fine.

25
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:24.084
But one query in a hundred hits a cold reranker and takes thirty seconds.

26
00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:26.733
A mean-only view hides exactly that pain.

27
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:30.067
Putting it together for your RAG endpoint.

28
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:33.833
The mean says one point four seconds, so everything looks fine.

29
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:37.500
But a few thirty second calls are hiding inside that number.

30
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.000
So report percentiles, which is exactly what the next topic is about.

31
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:43.913
Let's recap.

32
00:01:43.913 --> 00:01:46.467
The mean is the balance point of your data.

33
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:49.400
One outlier can drag it arbitrarily far.

34
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.607
Use it for symmetric data, and be careful with skewed data.

35
00:01:53.607 --> 00:01:54.667
Next up: Median.
